<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Tapajó</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1640 CE (approx.) · capital Santarém</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A large chiefdom at the meeting of the Tapajós and Amazon rivers, known for its intricate pottery and dark earth soils.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Tapajó people dominated the lower Tapajós river around the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-para">Santarém</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> from about 1000. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem">Santarém</a> culture associated with them developed from earlier Amazonian traditions, and its main settlement covered several square kilometres.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Spanish and Portuguese explorers in the 16th and 17th centuries described a numerous people under powerful chiefs who could field many warriors armed with poisoned arrows. The Tapajó farmed on terra preta, fertile dark earth created by long settlement, and fished in the rivers. Their potters made elaborate caryatid vessels and vases decorated with modelled animals and figures, and they carved green stone pendants called muiraquitãs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Portuguese slaving expeditions from the 1620s and the founding of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission in 1661, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-para">Santarém</a>, ended Tapajó independence. Epidemics and enslavement destroyed most of the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem">Santarém</a> ceramic style is today among the best known Amazonian archaeological traditions.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1000 CE – 1640 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.60%;width:12.74%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1640 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santarém</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancestor worship</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tapajós, Santarém culture</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1320&amp;focus=tapajo" aria-label="Tapajó on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3161 2269 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tapajó lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3481 2525L3944 2917"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3481 2525h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3481 2525h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santarém. <a href="/atlas?year=1320&amp;focus=tapajo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

